== Chapter 16

#align(center)[THE WORLD, CREATED BY GOD, STILL NOURISHED AND PROTECTED BY HIS POWER. EACH AND ALL OF ITS PARTS GOVERNED BY HIS PROVIDENCE.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[#emph[Beveridge’s summary.] The divisions of this chapter are: I. The doctrine of the special providence of God over all created things, singly and collectively, as opposed to the dreams of the Epicureans about fortune and fortuitous causes. II. The fiction of the sophists about the omnipotence of God, and the error of the philosophers about a confused and ambiguous government of the world, sec. 1-5. All animals, but especially mankind, because of the special oversight exercised over them, are proofs, evidences, and examples of the providence of God, sec. 6, 7. III. A consideration of fate, fortune, chance, contingency, and uncertain events (on which the matter under discussion here turns).]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[#emph[Sections (Beveridge’s summary).]]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[1\. Even the wicked, guided by carnal sense, acknowledge that God is the Creator. The godly acknowledge not only this, but that he is a most wise and powerful governor and preserver of all created things. In this they rely on the Word of God, some passages of which are cited.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[2\. Refutation of the Epicureans, who set fortune and fortuitous causes against divine providence as Scripture teaches it. The sun, a bright display of divine providence.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[3\. The sophists’ fiction of an idle providence refuted. The omnipotence of God considered together with his providence. The double benefit of a right acknowledgment of the divine omnipotence. The quibbles of unbelief.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[4\. A definition of providence, refuting the erroneous dogmas of the philosophers. Dreams of the Epicureans and Peripatetics.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[5\. The special providence of God asserted and proved by arguments drawn from the divine justice and mercy. Proved also by passages of Scripture about the sky, the earth, and animals.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[6\. Special providence proved by passages about the human race, all the more because the world was created for its sake.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[7\. Special providence proved, lastly, by examples from the history of the Israelites, of Jonah, and of Jacob, and from daily experience.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[8\. Erroneous views of providence refuted: I. The sect of the Stoics. II. The fortune and chance of the heathen.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[9\. How things are said to be fortuitous to us, though done by the determinate counsel of God. An example. The error of separating contingency and outcome from the secret, but just and most wise, counsel of God. Two examples.]

1\. #smallcaps[It] would be cold and lifeless to represent God as a momentary Creator, who completed his work once for all and then left it. Here especially we must part company with the ungodly, and maintain that the presence of the divine power is conspicuous no less in the perpetual condition of the world than in its first creation. For although even wicked men are forced by the sight of earth and sky alone to rise to the Creator, faith has a method of its own in assigning the whole praise of creation to God. This is the point of the passage of the Apostle already quoted, that only by faith do we understand that the worlds were framed by the Word of God (Heb 11:3). For unless we go on to his providence, we cannot understand the full force of what it means that God is the Creator, however much we may seem to grasp it with our mind and confess it with our tongue. The carnal mind, once it has seen the power of God in the creation, stops there. At the most, it thinks and ponders on nothing but the wisdom, power, and goodness the Author shows in such a work (things that present themselves of their own accord and force themselves on the notice even of the unwilling), or on some general agency, exercised in preserving and governing it, on which the power of motion depends. In short, it imagines that all things are sufficiently sustained by the energy divinely infused into them at the first. But faith must go deeper. Having learned that he is the Creator of all, it must at once conclude that he is also their perpetual Governor and Preserver. And he is this, not by producing a kind of general motion in the machine of the world and in each of its parts, but by a special providence sustaining, nourishing, and watching over everything he has made, down to the very least, even to a sparrow. So David, after stating briefly at the outset that the world was created by God, at once comes down to the continual course of providence: “By the word of the #smallcaps[Lord] the heavens were made, and all the stars by the breath of His mouth”; and he adds at once, “The #smallcaps[Lord] looks down from heaven; He sees all the sons of men” (Ps 33:6, 13, etc.). He goes on to add other things to the same effect. For although not everyone reasons so precisely, yet because it would not be credible that God took care of human affairs unless he were the maker of the world, and no one seriously believes that he is its Creator without being persuaded that he takes care of his works; so David, with good reason and in admirable order, leads us from the one to the other. In general terms, indeed, philosophers teach, and the human mind conceives, that all the parts of the world are given life by the secret inspiration of God. Yet they do not reach the height to which David rises, taking all the godly with him, when he says: “All creatures look to You to give them their food in due season. When You give it to them, they gather it up; when You open Your hand, they are satisfied with good things. When You hide Your face, they are terrified; when You take away their breath, they die and return to dust. When You send Your Spirit, they are created, and You renew the face of the earth” (Ps 104:27–30). Indeed, though they subscribe to Paul’s saying that in God “we live and move and have our being” (Acts 17:28), they are far from any serious sense of the grace he commends, because they have not the least taste for that special care in which alone the fatherly favor of God is discerned.

2\. To make this distinction clearer, we must understand that the providence of God, as Scripture teaches it, is set against fortune and fortuitous causes. An erroneous opinion has prevailed in every age, and almost universally prevails in our own day: namely, that all things happen fortuitously. By it the true doctrine of providence has been not only obscured but almost buried. If a man falls among robbers or ravenous beasts; if a sudden gust of wind at sea causes a shipwreck; if a man is struck down by the fall of a house or a tree; if another, wandering through desert places, finds deliverance; or, after being tossed by the waves, reaches port and makes some wonderful hair’s-breadth escape from death—carnal sense will attribute all these events, prosperous as well as adverse, to fortune. But whoever has learned from the mouth of Christ that all the hairs of his head are numbered (Matt 10:30) will look further for the cause, and will hold that all events whatever are governed by the secret counsel of God. Again, with regard to inanimate things, we must hold that though each has its own properties, they all exert their force only insofar as they are directed by the immediate hand of God. So they are merely instruments, into which God constantly infuses as much energy as he sees fit, and which he bends and turns to any purpose he pleases. No created thing makes a more wonderful or glorious display than the sun. For besides illuminating the whole world with its brightness, how wonderfully it nourishes and invigorates all animals by its heat, and fertilizes the earth by its rays, warming the seeds of grain in its lap and so calling forth the green blade! This blade it supports, increases, and strengthens with fresh nourishment until it rises into the stalk; it goes on feeding it with constant moisture until it comes into flower, and from flower to fruit, which it keeps ripening until it is fully mature. In the same way, by its warmth trees and vines bud and put forth first their leaves, then their blossom, then their fruit. But the Lord, to claim the entire glory of all this as his own, willed that light should exist, and that the earth should be filled with every kind of plant and fruit, before he made the sun (Gen 1:3, 11). No godly man, therefore, will make the sun either the necessary or the principal cause of the things that existed before the sun was created, but only the instrument God uses because he so wills, though he could set it aside and act just as well by himself. Again, when we read that at the prayer of Joshua the sun stood still in one degree for two days (Josh 10:13), and that as a favor to Hezekiah its shadow went back ten degrees (2 Kgs 20:11), by these miracles God declared that the sun does not rise and set each day by a blind instinct of nature, but is governed by Him in its course, so that he may renew the remembrance of his fatherly favor toward us. Nothing is more natural than for spring to follow winter in its turn, summer spring, and autumn summer. Yet in this series the variations are so great and so unequal that it is plain that every single year, month, and day is regulated by a new and special providence of God.

3\. And truly God claims omnipotence for himself and wants us to acknowledge it—not the vain, indolent, slumbering omnipotence the sophists imagine, but a watchful, efficacious, energetic omnipotence, always in action; not an omnipotence that acts only as a general principle of confused motion, as if he ordered a river to keep within the channel once laid down for it, but one that is intent on individual and special movements. God is deemed omnipotent, not because he can indeed act, yet meanwhile ceases and sits idle, or because by a general instinct he continues the order of nature he appointed beforehand, but because, governing heaven and earth by his providence, he so overrules all things that nothing happens without his counsel. For when the psalm says, “He does as He pleases” (Ps 115:3), what is meant is his sure and deliberate purpose. It would be insipid to interpret the psalmist’s words in the philosophers’ way, as meaning that God is the primary agent because he is the beginning and cause of all motion. Rather, this is the comfort of the faithful in their adversity: that everything they endure comes by the ordination and command of God, for they are under his hand. But if God’s government extends in this way to all his works, it is a childish quibble to confine it to the influence of nature.#footnote[See Hyperius in Methodo Theologiæ. (Beveridge’s note.)] Moreover, those who confine the providence of God within such narrow limits, as if he let all things be carried along freely by a perpetual law of nature, rob God of his glory, and themselves no less of a most useful doctrine; for nothing would be more wretched than man if he were exposed to every movement of the sky, the air, the earth, and the waters. Add to this that in this way the singular goodness of God toward each individual is most unworthily diminished. David exclaims (Ps 8:3) that infants still hanging at their mothers’ breasts are eloquent enough to celebrate the glory of God, because from the very moment of their birth they find food prepared for them by heavenly care. This is indeed true in general; but if we do not shut our eyes and senses to what experience plainly shows, we must see that some mothers have full provision for their infants and others almost none, as it pleases God to nourish one child more generously and another more sparingly. Those who give due praise to the omnipotence of God derive a double benefit from it. First, he to whom heaven and earth belong, and to whose nod all creatures look, so as to devote themselves to his service, has ample power to do good. Secondly, they can rest secure in the protection of Him to whose control everything that could harm them is subject; by whose authority Satan, with all his furies and all his devices, is curbed as with a bridle; and on whose will everything opposed to our safety depends. In this way, and in no other, can the immoderate and superstitious fears stirred up by the dangers we are exposed to be calmed or subdued. I say superstitious fears. For such they are whenever the dangers threatened by any created things fill us with such terror that we tremble as if they had in themselves the power to hurt us, or could hurt us at random or by chance, or as if we did not have in God sufficient protection against them. For example, Jeremiah forbids the children of God to “be terrified by the signs in the heavens, though the nations themselves are terrified by them” (Jer 10:2). He does not, indeed, condemn every kind of fear. But because unbelievers transfer the government of the world from God to the stars, imagining that their happiness or misery depends on the stars’ decrees and omens and not on the will of God, it follows that their fear, which ought to look to him alone, is turned aside to stars and comets. Let the man, then, who would guard against such unbelief always bear in mind that there is no random power, or action, or motion in created things, but that they are so governed by the secret counsel of God that nothing happens except what he has knowingly and willingly decreed.#footnote[See Calvin adversus Astrolog. Judiciariam. August De Ordine, lib. 2 cap. 15. (Beveridge’s note.)]

4\. First, then, let the reader remember that the providence we mean is not one by which the Deity sits idly in heaven and looks on at what is happening in the world, but one by which he holds the helm, as it were, and overrules all events. So his providence extends no less to the hand than to the eye.#footnote[The French adds, “C’est à dire, que non seulement il voit, mais aussi ordonne ce qu’il veut estre fait;”—“that is to say, he not only sees, but ordains what he wills to be done.” (Beveridge’s note.)] When Abraham said to his son, #emph[God will provide] (Gen 22:8), he meant not merely to assert that God foreknew what would happen, but to commit the care of a matter he could not see the end of to the will of Him whose part it is to bring perplexing and doubtful matters to a happy outcome. So it follows that providence lies in action. The talk of many about bare foreknowledge is the merest trifling. Those who attribute government to God do not err quite so grossly; but still, as I have said, it is a confused and indiscriminate government, which consists in giving an impulse and a general movement to the machine of the world and each of its parts, but does not specially direct the action of each creature. This error, however, cannot be tolerated either. For its supporters say that nothing in this providence, which they call universal, prevents all created things from moving contingently, or man from turning himself this way or that by the pure freedom of his own will. In this way they make man a partner with God: God by his power gives man the movement by which he can act in keeping with the nature conferred on him, while man himself regulates his own actions by his own will. In short, they hold that the world, human affairs, and men themselves are governed by God’s power but not by his decree. I say nothing of the Epicureans (a plague the world has always been full of), who dream of an inert and idle God,#footnote[Plin. lib. 2. c. 7. “Irridendum vero, agere curam rerum humanarum, illud, quicquid est, summum. Anne tam tristi atque multiplici ministerio non pollui credamus dubitemusve?” \[But it is laughable that the supreme being, whatever it is, should take care of human affairs. Can we believe, or even doubt, that it would not be defiled by so grim and manifold a ministry?\] (Beveridge’s note.)] and of others no sounder, who in former times made up the idea that God rules the upper regions of the air but leaves the lower ones to Fortune. Against such obvious madness even mute creatures raise their voice.

My aim now is to refute an opinion that has become very widespread: one that, while it grants God some blind and ambiguous movement, withholds what matters most, namely, that he disposes and directs everything to its proper end by incomprehensible wisdom. By withholding government, it makes God the ruler of the world in name only, not in reality. For what, I ask, does it mean to govern, if not to preside in such a way that you rule what you preside over by a fixed order? I do not, however, wholly reject what is said of a universal providence, provided that it is granted to me in turn that the world is governed by God, not only because he maintains the order of nature he has appointed, but because he takes special care of each one of his works. It is true, indeed, that each species of created things is moved by a secret instinct of nature, as if they obeyed the eternal command of God and followed of their own accord the course God appointed at the first. And to this we may refer our Savior’s words, that he and his Father have always been at work from the beginning (John 5:17); also Paul’s words, that “in Him we live and move and have our being” (Acts 17:28); and also the words of the author of the Epistle to the Hebrews, who, wanting to prove the divinity of Christ, says that he upholds “all things by His powerful word” (Heb 1:3). But some, under cover of the general providence, hide and obscure the special providence, which is taught in Scripture so surely and clearly that it is strange anyone could bring himself to doubt it. And indeed, those who put up that screen are themselves forced to amend their doctrine by adding that many things are done by the special care of God. But this they wrongly confine to particular acts. What we must prove, then, is that single events are so regulated by God, and all events so proceed from his determinate counsel, that nothing happens fortuitously.

5\. Suppose we grant that the beginning of motion belongs to God, but that all things move of themselves or by chance, as the impulse of nature drives them. Then the alternation of day and night, of summer and winter, will be the work of God, insofar as he assigned each its part and appointed a fixed law; that is, it would be so if they always kept the same course with uniform regularity, day following night, month following month, and year following year. But when at one time excessive heat combined with drought burns up the fields, and at another excessive rains rot the crops, and sudden devastation comes from tempests and hailstorms, these will not be the works of God, except insofar as rainy or fair weather, heat or cold, arise from the conjunction of the stars and other natural causes. On this view no room is left either for God’s fatherly favor or for his judgments. If they say that God shows his kindness to the human race fully by giving heaven and earth the ordinary power of producing food, the explanation is thin and heathenish: as if the fruitfulness of one year were not a special blessing, and the scarcity and famine of another a special punishment and curse from God. But since it would take too long to list all the arguments, let the authority of God himself be enough. In the Law and the Prophets he declares again and again that whenever he waters the earth with dew and rain he shows his favor; that at his command the sky becomes hard as iron and the crops are destroyed by mildew and other blights; and that storms and hail, when they ravage the fields, are signs of his sure and special vengeance. If we accept this, it is certain that not a drop of rain falls without God’s express command. David, indeed (Ps 147:9), praises the general providence of God in giving food to the young ravens that cry to him; but when God himself threatens living creatures with famine, does he not plainly declare that he feeds them all, at one time with a scanty measure, at another with a more ample one, as he sees fit? It is childish, as I have already said, to confine this to particular acts, when Christ says without any exception that not a sparrow falls to the ground without the will of his Father (Matt 10:29). Surely, if the flight of birds is regulated by the counsel of God, we must acknowledge with the prophet that while he is “enthroned on high,” he “humbles Himself to behold” the things that are in heaven and on earth (Ps 113:5, 6).

6\. But since we know that the world was made chiefly for the sake of mankind, we must look to this as the end God has in view in governing it as well. The prophet Jeremiah exclaims, “I know, O #smallcaps[Lord], that a man’s way is not his own; no one who walks directs his own steps” (Jer 10:23). Solomon, again, says, “A man’s steps are from the #smallcaps[Lord], so how can anyone understand his own way?” (Prov 20:24). Will they now say that man is moved by God according to the bent of his nature, but that man himself turns that movement in any direction he pleases? If that were truly so, man would have full control of his own ways. Perhaps they will answer that man can do nothing without the power of God. But that answer will not help them, since both Jeremiah and Solomon attribute to God not only power but also choice and decree. And elsewhere Solomon neatly rebukes the rashness of men who fix their plans without regard to God, as if they were not led by his hand: “The ordering of the heart belongs to man, and the preparing of the tongue to the #smallcaps[Lord]” (Prov 16:1). It is surely a strange madness for miserable men, who cannot even speak a word except as God pleases, to set about acting without him! Moreover, to show more clearly that everything done in the world is done according to his decree, Scripture declares that the things that seem most fortuitous are subject to him. For what seems more a matter of chance than a branch that falls from a tree and kills a passing traveler? But the Lord sees it very differently, and declares that He delivered him into the hand of the slayer (Exod 21:13). In the same way, who does not attribute the lot to the blindness of Fortune? Not so the Lord, who claims the decision for himself (Prov 16:33). He does not say that by his power the lot is thrown into the lap and drawn out, but declares that the one thing that could be credited to chance comes from him. To the same effect are Solomon’s words: “The poor man and the usurer meet together; the #smallcaps[Lord] gives light to the eyes of both” (Prov 29:13). For although rich and poor are mixed together in the world, in saying that each one’s condition is appointed by God, Solomon reminds us that God, who gives light to all, keeps his own eye always open; and so he urges the poor to patient endurance, since those who are discontented with their lot are trying to throw off a burden God has laid on them. So too another prophet rebukes ungodly men who credit human effort or fortune with the fact that some lie in the mire while others rise to honor: “For exaltation comes neither from east nor west, nor out of the desert, but it is God who judges; He brings down one and exalts another” (Ps 75:6, 7). Because God cannot lay aside the office of judge, the prophet reasons that it is owing to his secret counsel that some are raised up while others remain without honor.

7\. Indeed, I affirm in general that particular events are evidences of the special providence of God. In the wilderness God caused a south wind to blow and brought the people a plentiful supply of birds (Exod 16:13; Num 11:31). When he wanted Jonah to be thrown into the sea, he sent forth a whirlwind (Jonah 1:4). Those who deny that God holds the reins of government will say that this was out of the ordinary; but I infer from it that no wind ever rises or rages without his special command. Nor could it be true in any other way that “He makes the winds His messengers, flames of fire His servants,” and that “He makes the clouds His chariot and walks on the wings of the wind” (Ps 104:3, 4), unless he drove the clouds and winds as he pleased and showed in them the special presence of his power. In the same way we are taught elsewhere that whenever the sea is raised into a storm, its waves bear witness to the special presence of God: “He spoke and raised a tempest that lifted the waves of the sea.” “He calmed the storm to a whisper, and the waves of the sea were hushed” (Ps 107:25, 29). Elsewhere too he declares that he had struck the people with burning winds (Amos 4:9). Again, although man by nature has the power of continuing his kind, God describes it as a mark of his special favor that some remain childless while others are blessed with offspring; for the fruit of the womb is his gift. Hence Jacob’s words to Rachel: “Am I in the place of God, who has withheld from you the fruit of the womb?” (Gen 30:2). To sum up in a word: nothing in nature is more ordinary than for us to be nourished by bread. Yet the Spirit declares not only that the produce of the earth is God’s special gift, but that “man does not live on bread alone” (Deut 8:3), because it is not fullness itself that nourishes him, but the secret blessing of God. And so, on the other hand, he threatens to take away “both supply and support: the whole supply of bread and water” (Isa 3:1). Indeed, our prayer for daily bread could have no serious meaning if God did not supply us with food by his fatherly hand. Therefore, to assure the faithful that in feeding them God fills the office of the best of parents, the prophet reminds them that he “gives food to every creature” (Ps 136:25). Finally, when we hear on the one hand that “the eyes of the #smallcaps[Lord] are on the righteous, and His ears are inclined to their cry,” and on the other that “the face of the #smallcaps[Lord] is against those who do evil, to wipe out all memory of them from the earth” (Ps 34:15, 16), let us be sure that all created things above and below stand ready for his service, so that he may use them in whatever way he pleases. From this we conclude not only that the general providence of God, continuing the order of nature, extends over created things, but that by his wonderful counsel they are fitted to a definite and special purpose.

8\. Those who want to bring this doctrine into disrepute slander it as the Stoics’ dogma of fate. The same charge was once brought against Augustine.#footnote[Lib. ad Bonifac. 2, c. 6 et alibi.] We have no wish to quarrel about words; but we do not accept the term Fate, both because it is one of those words whose godless novelty Paul teaches us to shun (1 Tim 6:20), and because our opponents try to fasten a stigma on the truth of God by means of a hated term. But the dogma itself is falsely and maliciously laid to our charge. For we do not, with the Stoics, imagine a necessity arising from a perpetual chain of causes and a kind of tangled series contained in nature. We hold that God is the arbiter and ruler of all things: that from the remotest eternity, according to his own wisdom, he decreed what he would do, and that now by his power he carries out what he decreed. So we maintain that by his providence not only heaven and earth and inanimate things, but also the plans and wills of men, are so governed that they move exactly in the course he has appointed. What then, you will say: does nothing happen fortuitously, nothing contingently? I answer that Basil the Great said truly that Fortune and Chance are heathen terms, whose meaning ought not to occupy godly minds. For if all success is God’s blessing, and calamity and adversity are his curse, no room is left in human affairs for fortune or chance. We ought also to be moved by the words of Augustine,#footnote[Retract. lib. 1 cap. 1.] “In my books against the Academics,” he says, “I regret having so often used the word Fortune, although I meant by it not some goddess, but the fortuitous outcome of events in outward things, whether good or evil. Hence, too, those words, Perhaps, Possibly, Fortuitously,#footnote[Forte. Forsan. Forsitan, Fortuito. (Beveridge’s note.)] which no religion forbids us to use, though everything must be referred to divine providence. Nor did I fail to note this when I said, Although perhaps what is commonly called Fortune is also governed by a hidden order, and what we call Chance is nothing but that whose reason and cause are secret. I did indeed say this; but I repent of having named Fortune there as I did, when I see the very bad habit men have of saying, not as they ought, ‘This is what God willed,’ but ‘This is what Fortune willed.’ ” In short, Augustine everywhere teaches that if anything is left to fortune, the world moves at random. And although he says elsewhere#footnote[Quæstionum, lib. 83.] that all things are carried on partly by the free will of man and partly by the providence of God, he shows clearly enough a little later that he meant that men too are ruled by providence, when he takes it as a principle that nothing could be more absurd than to hold that anything is done without God’s ordaining it, because then it would happen at random. For this reason he also excludes the contingency that depends on human will, and a little further on he says in clearer terms that we must not look for any cause of the will of God. When he uses the term permission, what he means by it will best appear from a single passage,#footnote[De Trinit. lib. 3 cap. 4.] where he proves that the will of God is the supreme and primary cause of all things, because nothing happens without his order or permission. When God chooses to permit anything, Augustine certainly does not picture him sitting idle in a watchtower. The will he represents as stepping in is, if I may put it so, active (#emph[actualis]); otherwise it could not be regarded as a cause.

9\. But since our sluggish minds lie far below the height of divine providence, we must use a distinction that may help them rise. I say, then, that though all things are ordered by the counsel and sure arrangement of God, to us they are fortuitous. This is not because we imagine that Fortune rules the world and mankind and turns all things upside down at random (far be such a senseless thought from every Christian heart). It is because the order, method, end, and necessity of events are for the most part hidden in the counsel of God and are not grasped by human opinion; and so, though it is certain that they are produced by the will of God, they have the appearance of being fortuitous. For that is the form in which they present themselves to us, whether they are considered in their own nature or weighed by our knowledge and judgment. Suppose, for example, that a merchant enters a forest in the company of trustworthy men, carelessly strays from his companions, and wanders lost until he falls into a robbers’ den and is murdered. His death was not only foreseen by the eye of God, but had been fixed by his decree. For it is said, not that he foresaw how far each man’s life would extend, but that he determined and fixed the bounds that could not be passed (Job 14:5). Yet as far as our minds can grasp, all these things appear fortuitous. What will the Christian think here? He will regard every circumstance of such a death as indeed fortuitous by its nature; yet he will have no doubt that the providence of God overruled it and directed fortune to God’s own end. The same holds for future contingencies. All future events, being uncertain to us, seem to hang in suspense, as if ready to go either way. Yet the conviction remains fixed in our hearts that nothing will happen that the Lord has not already foreseen and provided. In this sense Ecclesiastes repeatedly uses the word “outcome,” because at first sight men do not see through to the primary cause, which lies hidden. And yet what Scripture teaches about the secret providence of God was never so completely erased from the human heart that some sparks did not always shine in the darkness. So the soothsayers of the Philistines, though they waver in doubt, attribute their disaster partly to God and partly to chance. If the ark, they say, “goes up the road to its homeland, toward Beth-shemesh, it is the #smallcaps[Lord] who has brought on us this great disaster. But if it does not, then we will know that it was not His hand that punished us and that it happened by chance” (1 Sam 6:9). Foolishly, indeed, when their divination fails them they flee to fortune. Yet we see them so constrained that they dare not regard their disaster as fortuitous. But how God, by the bridle of his providence, turns events in whatever direction he pleases will be clear from a remarkable example. At the very moment when David had been trapped in the wilderness of Maon, the Philistines invaded the land, and Saul was forced to withdraw (1 Sam 23:26, 27). If God, wishing to provide for his servant’s safety, threw this obstacle in Saul’s way, then, though the Philistines took up arms against all human expectation, we surely cannot say that they did it by chance. What seems to us contingency, faith will recognize as the secret impulse of God. The reason is not always so plain; but we must hold without any doubt that all the changes that take place in the world come from the secret working of the hand of God. At the same time, what God has determined, though it must come to pass, is not, however, necessary strictly speaking, or in its own nature. We have a familiar example in our Savior’s bones. Since he took on a body like ours, no sane man will deny that his bones could be broken; and yet it was impossible that they should be broken (John 19:33, 36). Here again we see that there was good reason for the distinctions the schoolmen made between necessity #emph[secundum quid] \[in a certain respect\] and absolute necessity, and also between the necessity of the #emph[consequent] and of the #emph[consequence]. God made his Son’s bones breakable, though he exempted them from actual breaking; and so, by the necessity of his counsel, he made impossible what could naturally have happened.
